WEBVTT

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:01.550
Hello everyone.

00:00:01.795 --> 00:00:05.070
Today I want to talk to you about a task that many people take lightly:

00:00:05.314 --> 00:00:06.270
gel removal.

00:00:06.896 --> 00:00:08.830
Everyone wants to learn to paint beautifully.

00:00:09.013 --> 00:00:13.150
Removal, people tend to see as a side job, something to get done quickly.

00:00:13.577 --> 00:00:14.750
But to be honest with you:

00:00:15.006 --> 00:00:17.246
when the nails of a client get damaged,

00:00:17.196 --> 00:00:20.110
most of the time it happens not during application,

00:00:20.269 --> 00:00:21.230
but during removal.

00:00:21.777 --> 00:00:24.527
There is a story we hear very often in this trade.

00:00:25.017 --> 00:00:28.527
A client sits down, holds out her hands, and says:

00:00:29.275 --> 00:00:32.607
my nails have been so weak lately, it must be the gel.

00:00:33.194 --> 00:00:37.274
Ask a little more, and it turns out that last time, sitting at home,

00:00:37.224 --> 00:00:39.407
she saw a corner of the gel lifting,

00:00:39.594 --> 00:00:40.927
and just peeled it all off.

00:00:41.427 --> 00:00:44.767
Or a technician in a hurry scraped it off with a pusher to save time.

00:00:45.006 --> 00:00:48.527
In most cases, it is not the gel that damages the nail.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:50.767
It is the wrong way of removing it.

00:00:51.377 --> 00:00:53.490
Why is peeling gel so harmful?

00:00:54.145 --> 00:00:57.764
The nail plate is made of keratin, in very many thin layers,

00:00:57.714 --> 00:00:59.489
stacked on top of each other,

00:00:59.521 --> 00:01:00.850
like the pages of a book.

00:01:01.472 --> 00:01:04.026
Cured gel bonds very firmly to the top layer,

00:01:03.976 --> 00:01:07.810
often more firmly than the layers of the nail hold on to each other.

00:01:08.156 --> 00:01:11.330
So when you peel, the gel does not come away from the nail.

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:14.290
It pulls several layers of the natural nail with it.

00:01:14.633 --> 00:01:18.690
The nail is left with white, rough patches, thin and weak.

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:22.221
And a nail that has become thin cannot thicken again.

00:01:22.694 --> 00:01:24.381
The nail plate is dead tissue;

00:01:24.489 --> 00:01:25.501
it does not heal.

00:01:25.917 --> 00:01:28.861
We can only wait for new nail to grow out and replace it.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:32.941
And fingernails grow slowly, only about three millimeters a month.

00:01:33.465 --> 00:01:37.101
That means it takes four to six months to have a completely new nail.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:41.341
One minute of quick peeling, and the client pays for it for half a year.

00:01:41.958 --> 00:01:46.475
To remove gel correctly, you first need to know what kind of gel is on the nail.

00:01:46.858 --> 00:01:47.995
There are two groups.

00:01:48.201 --> 00:01:50.235
One group dissolves in acetone:

00:01:50.499 --> 00:01:53.515
most gel polishes, and some soft builder gels.

00:01:53.985 --> 00:01:57.115
With this group, we soak the gel until it softens.

00:01:57.397 --> 00:01:58.955
The other group is hard gel.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:02.555
Acetone cannot break it down, however long you soak it.

00:02:02.838 --> 00:02:04.395
We have to file it down thin.

00:02:04.639 --> 00:02:09.091
If a client comes from another salon, and you do not know what it is,

00:02:09.041 --> 00:02:11.835
ask the client, and test on one nail first.

00:02:12.024 --> 00:02:14.714
And always read the removal instructions of the brand.

00:02:15.309 --> 00:02:19.359
With gel that dissolves, there is one step new technicians often skip:

00:02:19.623 --> 00:02:20.799
breaking the top coat.

00:02:21.466 --> 00:02:24.319
The top coat is made to resist chemicals and scratches.

00:02:24.581 --> 00:02:26.319
It is the raincoat of the whole set.

00:02:26.654 --> 00:02:31.786
With the top coat intact, acetone cannot get in, and after half an hour of soaking,

00:02:31.736 --> 00:02:33.439
the gel is still untouched.

00:02:33.751 --> 00:02:37.039
Then the technician gets impatient, and scrapes it off with a pusher.

00:02:37.278 --> 00:02:42.239
So, use a 180 grit file, and file lightly until all the shine is gone.

00:02:42.507 --> 00:02:45.999
File only the top coat, and stop when you see colored dust.

00:02:46.151 --> 00:02:47.999
Do not file into the natural nail.

00:02:48.595 --> 00:02:49.925
Then comes soaking.

00:02:50.262 --> 00:02:54.285
A piece of cotton soaked in acetone, placed exactly on the nail,

00:02:54.235 --> 00:02:56.085
then wrapped tightly in foil,

00:02:56.115 --> 00:02:57.125
or held with a clip.

00:02:57.711 --> 00:03:00.938
Usually ten to fifteen minutes, but every brand is different,

00:03:00.888 --> 00:03:03.125
so follow the instructions of the product.

00:03:03.554 --> 00:03:06.725
And here is the most important piece of experience in this lesson:

00:03:07.149 --> 00:03:09.765
let the chemical do the work, not your hands.

00:03:10.084 --> 00:03:13.525
When you open it, the gel should lift and crack by itself.

00:03:13.954 --> 00:03:17.125
A gentle push with a wooden stick, and the gel comes away.

00:03:17.545 --> 00:03:19.765
Where it still sticks, do not scrape.

00:03:20.169 --> 00:03:22.485
Wrap it again, and soak a few more minutes.

00:03:22.834 --> 00:03:25.845
Five more minutes of patience saves the nail of your client.

00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:30.904
As for hard gel, and removal with an electric file, I will say it plainly:

00:03:31.253 --> 00:03:34.584
the electric file is only for people who have had proper training.

00:03:35.093 --> 00:03:39.944
In unsteady hands, an electric file can cut into the natural nail within seconds.

00:03:40.292 --> 00:03:44.264
The client feels burning, and the nail is left with grooves and rings.

00:03:44.697 --> 00:03:46.664
The rule of those who do it right is:

00:03:46.911 --> 00:03:48.504
do not take it all off.

00:03:48.763 --> 00:03:51.704
Always leave a very thin base layer on the nail.

00:03:51.974 --> 00:03:55.144
That layer protects the natural nail, and grows out with it.

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:58.584
If you have not learned the electric file, file by hand.

00:03:58.931 --> 00:04:00.744
It is slower, but it is safe.

00:04:01.372 --> 00:04:02.582
Now about acetone.

00:04:03.026 --> 00:04:04.606
It removes gel very well,

00:04:04.556 --> 00:04:08.342
but it also strips oil and water from the skin and the nails.

00:04:08.828 --> 00:04:12.502
That is why, after removal, nails look white and dry.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:16.022
That is temporary dehydration, not damaged nails.

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:20.890
You can reduce it by applying oil or cream to the skin around the nail before soaking,

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:22.422
keeping it off the nail plate.

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:26.342
And use only a small piece of cotton, exactly the size of the nail.

00:04:26.549 --> 00:04:28.822
Do not let the chemical flood the whole finger.

00:04:29.465 --> 00:04:32.349
There are two things about acetone you must know by heart.

00:04:32.687 --> 00:04:33.309
First:

00:04:33.576 --> 00:04:36.909
it is highly flammable, and its vapor catches fire too.

00:04:37.546 --> 00:04:38.749
At the removal table:

00:04:38.778 --> 00:04:41.709
no candles, no flames, no smoking.

00:04:42.293 --> 00:04:45.229
Never warm it over a flame, or in a microwave.

00:04:45.718 --> 00:04:46.349
Second:

00:04:46.694 --> 00:04:50.349
in a closed room, its vapor causes headaches and dizziness.

00:04:50.698 --> 00:04:53.944
Open the ventilation, close the bottle right after use,

00:04:53.894 --> 00:04:56.269
and put used cotton in a bin with a lid.

00:04:56.795 --> 00:04:58.745
One more thing, for yourselves:

00:04:58.893 --> 00:05:00.425
dust, and gloves.

00:05:00.884 --> 00:05:03.865
When you file or drill gel, dust rises.

00:05:04.294 --> 00:05:08.983
If that gel was not fully cured, the dust still contains uncured material,

00:05:08.933 --> 00:05:10.425
which causes allergies.

00:05:11.009 --> 00:05:15.465
This kind of allergy comes slowly, but once you have it, it is usually for life.

00:05:15.738 --> 00:05:18.985
So wear gloves, and use a dust extractor if you have one.

00:05:19.415 --> 00:05:23.145
Do not brush the dust away with bare hands, and do not blow it.

00:05:23.251 --> 00:05:26.021
And nitrile gloves break down quickly in acetone,

00:05:25.971 --> 00:05:28.185
so change them as soon as they are wet.

00:05:28.832 --> 00:05:31.631
When the removal is done, the job is not over.

00:05:31.961 --> 00:05:35.151
This is the only moment you see the natural nail of your client.

00:05:35.563 --> 00:05:36.351
Look closely.

00:05:36.859 --> 00:05:40.116
If the nail is thin, soft, or sore when pressed,

00:05:40.066 --> 00:05:43.791
advise the client to take a break from gel for a while,

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:45.311
and use oil every day.

00:05:45.979 --> 00:05:47.071
Many people say:

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:48.991
nails need a break to breathe.

00:05:49.395 --> 00:05:51.391
In fact, nails do not breathe.

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:53.951
They are fed by blood, from underneath.

00:05:54.295 --> 00:05:58.031
Nails need a break so that the damaged part has time to grow out completely.

00:05:58.619 --> 00:06:02.340
But if you see the nail plate separating from the nail bed,

00:06:02.290 --> 00:06:04.269
a nail turning green or yellow,

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:10.474
thickening or crumbling, or the skin around the nail swollen, red, painful,

00:06:10.424 --> 00:06:12.349
or with pus, then stop.

00:06:12.686 --> 00:06:14.349
Do not paint over it to hide it.

00:06:14.844 --> 00:06:18.109
Talk gently to the client, and advise them to see a doctor.

00:06:18.525 --> 00:06:20.749
We are technicians, not doctors:

00:06:20.919 --> 00:06:23.469
we do not diagnose, and we do not treat.

00:06:23.718 --> 00:06:27.709
Saying no at the right moment protects the client, and the whole salon.

00:06:28.367 --> 00:06:29.150
On hygiene.

00:06:29.539 --> 00:06:33.150
Foil, cotton, wooden sticks, files:

00:06:33.419 --> 00:06:36.429
a new set for every client, thrown away after use.

00:06:37.097 --> 00:06:42.030
Metal pushers are washed, disinfected and sterilized, like every other metal tool.

00:06:42.697 --> 00:06:46.830
Reusable soaking clips must be cleaned and disinfected after every client.

00:06:47.338 --> 00:06:51.919
And the rules on chemicals, ventilation and waste differ from country to country,

00:06:51.869 --> 00:06:53.150
and from city to city.

00:06:53.321 --> 00:06:55.150
Ask the health authority where you work.

00:06:55.819 --> 00:06:56.530
You see.

00:06:56.915 --> 00:06:59.410
Gel removal does not need strong hands.

00:06:59.668 --> 00:07:01.890
It needs understanding, and patience.

00:07:02.399 --> 00:07:06.159
A good technician is one whose clients, after many years of gel,

00:07:06.109 --> 00:07:09.810
still have nails as thick and healthy as on the very first day.

00:07:10.236 --> 00:07:12.850
And now, take the short quiz below the video.

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:14.370
See you in the next lesson.
